<section image-layout="blank" id="c7sb37ekfqftmol"><div class="body"><h1 align="center">2026 Market Update: Top Takeaways</h1><p align="center">Redpoint Ventures — Internal Synthesis</p><hr><smart-layout variant="solidBoxes" cell-size="20"><smart-layout-item id="OV-rw"><h4>The Build-Out Is Real</h4><p>$700B+ hyperscaler capex in 2026 — structurally distinct from the dotcom era. Demand is cash-flow funded and pre-committed.</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item id="SLREK"><h4>Developers Are Not Being Replaced</h4><p>Software engineer job postings rebounding to 96% of Jan 2024 levels. AI is expanding the market, not shrinking it.</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item id="RO5eI"><h4>Valuations Are Near-Crisis Lows</h4><p>Public SaaS NTM multiples at 4.1x — near all-time lows. Horizontal SaaS down -35% LTM. Market is pricing ~1.1% terminal growth.</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item id="GVSk3"><h4>Moat Depth Determines Survival</h4><p>Vertical (+3%) and Infrastructure (+2%) are holding. Horizontal (-35%) is being repriced as structurally vulnerable.</p></smart-layout-item></smart-layout><smart-layout variant="solidBoxes" cell-size="20"><smart-layout-item id="xCzDE"><h4>CIOs Are Consolidating</h4><p>54% pursuing vendor consolidation. 45% of AI budgets replacing existing software spend. Only 3% expect more vendors.</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item id="wIKbl"><h4>AI-Native Is Compounding Fast</h4><p>OpenAI and Anthropic each generating $20B+ ARR. Seed valuations spiked to ~$60M median in Q1'26. Top 20 deals = 44% of all VC dollars.</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item id="LtdKu"><h4>2026–2027: The Founding Window</h4><p>Historical data shows companies founded ~4 years post-platform shift capture the most cumulative enterprise value. The window is now.</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item id="_z_2H"><h4>Actionable Playbook</h4><p>Two paths for AI-native startups. Eight imperatives for incumbents. The terminal value repricing is happening before fundamentals break.</p></smart-layout-item></smart-layout></div></section><section image-layout="blank" id="e7frzg26ut77j66"><div class="body"><labels><label variant="solid">Chapter 1</label></labels><h1>The AI Build-Out Is Real — and Nothing Like the Dotcom Bubble</h1><img id="Joiw_" src="https://cdn.gamma.app/zc87vhr30n8uf3n/uploaded-images/P9d8BCvdc3m4XqNtvP7Pu.png" source="image.custom" resize="{&quot;width&quot;:760}"><columns col-widths="[58,42]"><div background-color="#1f2a5d"><h4>Scale of Capital Commitment</h4><p>Estimated hyperscaler data center capex in 2026 alone exceeds <span text-color="#fac496"><b>$700B+</b></span> — more than double the $250B+ spent on communications infrastructure at the 2000 dotcom peak. This is not speculative deployment. More than <span text-color="#fac496"><b>90% of build-out capacity is pre-committed</b></span> before ground breaks, with near-zero data center vacancy rates across major markets. Physical scarcity constraints — power, land, interconnect — make speculative overbuild structurally difficult to fund.</p><p>Hyperscaler CapEx CAGR from 2024A to 2027E: <b>Overall 36%</b> — with Meta at <b>54%</b>, Google at <b>49%</b>, Microsoft at <b>33%</b>, Amazon at <b>22%</b>, and Other at <b>32%</b>. These are not one-year spikes; they are multi-year commitments embedded in earnings guidance.</p></div><div><h4>Demand Is Cash-Flow Funded — Not Speculative</h4><p>The 2000 bubble was built on eyeballs and future promises. Today's build-out is funded by real, compounding revenue. <b>OpenAI and Anthropic are each generating $20B+ ARR</b>. Nvidia's EPS stands at approximately <b>$4.06</b> — vs. Cisco's $0.37 at the 2000 peak, an 11x gap in underlying earnings power.</p><p>On a macro trajectory basis, AI investment as a share of US GDP is on track to rival the railroad and internet buildout waves — with the bull-case scenario projecting AI software, hardware, and infrastructure reaching <b>~3.3% of US GDP by 2030</b>.</p><aside variant="success"><p>The structural conclusion: this is a demand-driven capital cycle, not a confidence-driven one. The risk of a Dotcom-style implosion is structurally lower.</p></aside></div></columns><graph chart-type="bar" show-data-labels="true" y-axis-min="auto" text-scale="1" dimensions="{&quot;width&quot;:1051.207729468599,&quot;height&quot;:588.5438233264321}"><properties><property key="company" label="Company" type="label"></property><property key="cagr" label="CapEx CAGR 2024–2027E (%)" type="data"></property></properties><data><row><cell>Meta</cell><cell>54</cell></row><row><cell>Google</cell><cell>49</cell></row><row><cell>Other</cell><cell>32</cell></row><row><cell>Overall</cell><cell>36</cell></row><row><cell>Microsoft</cell><cell>33</cell></row><row><cell>Amazon</cell><cell>22</cell></row></data></graph></div></section><section image-layout="blank" id="serjvwqdqrz1rdm"><div class="body"><labels><label variant="solid">Chapter 2</label></labels><h1>AI Is Expanding Software Creation — Not Replacing Developers</h1><img id="oVRzj" src="https://cdn.gamma.app/zc87vhr30n8uf3n/uploaded-images/KT7JlRStd0zpflkQXJckt.png" source="image.custom" resize="{&quot;width&quot;:760}"><columns col-widths="[50,50]"><div><h4>Output Metrics Are Accelerating, Not Contracting</h4><p>Counter to the displacement narrative, every measurable output of software creation is growing sharply. <b>New websites are growing ~40% YoY</b> by 2026. New iOS app submissions are up <b>40%+ YoY</b> in early 2025. GitHub code volume is up <b>~35% YoY</b> by 2025. These are not AI-generated noise — they represent real product creation activity.</p><p>Most telling: <b>software engineer job postings have rebounded to 96% of January 2024 levels</b> by January 2026, meaningfully outpacing total Indeed job postings which sit at only 85% of the same baseline. The labor market data directly contradicts the displacement thesis.</p><aside variant="info"><p>Historical precedent supports this: ATMs were predicted to eliminate 75% of bank tellers after 1973. Instead, teller employment grew <b>81% from 1970–1988</b> as lower branch operating costs enabled rapid branch expansion. Automation lowered the marginal cost and expanded the market.</p></aside></div><div><h4>Agent Maturity Curve: Where We Actually Are</h4><p>The agent revolution is real but early-stage. Today we are largely at the <b>Task Agent</b> phase — AI executes discrete tasks end-to-end with human oversight, independent runtime measured in minutes. The addressable market at this stage is estimated at <b>~$1.2T TAM</b>.</p><p>The larger opportunities sit ahead: <b>Workflow Agents</b> (~$2.8T TAM) — AI orchestrating multi-step business processes across systems with runtimes measured in hours — and <b>Autonomous Agents</b> (~$6.1T+ TAM) — AI independently performing complex knowledge work over days with minimal human oversight.</p><p>Why did coding come first? Four structural reasons: instant binary verification, 100B+ lines of GitHub training data (the richest structured dataset ever assembled), clearly defined syntax constraints that bound the output space, and a self-improving loop where AI writes code that trains the next generation of models.</p></div></columns><smart-layout variant="arrows" cell-size="15"><smart-layout-item id="hlmIB"><h4>Task Agents</h4><p><b>~$1.2T TAM</b> · Minutes of runtime · Human oversight · <i>Where we are today</i></p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item id="3s5_-"><h4>Workflow Agents</h4><p><b>~$2.8T TAM</b> · Hours of runtime · Multi-step orchestration · <i>Near-term frontier</i></p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item id="VPM7b"><h4>Autonomous Agents</h4><p><b>~$6.1T+ TAM</b> · Days of runtime · Minimal oversight · <i>The long-horizon prize</i></p></smart-layout-item></smart-layout></div></section><section image-layout="blank" id="rsrn1l9cu66x20e"><div class="body"><labels><label variant="solid">Chapter 3</label></labels><h1>Software Stocks Are Pricing in a Structural Collapse — The Market May Be Overreacting</h1><columns col-widths="[72,28]"><div><img id="2xLkO" src="https://cdn.gamma.app/zc87vhr30n8uf3n/uploaded-images/yPx6VSOWZjfRKPL5Wa2N7.png" source="image.custom"></div><div><p></p></div></columns><columns col-widths="[45,55]"><div background-color="#12386e"><h4>2026 YTD Sector Performance</h4><smart-layout variant="stats" cell-size="10"><smart-layout-item id="wgCQZ" label="-20%"><h4>Software</h4><p>Worst-performing S&amp;P 500 sector in 2026 YTD</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item id="dYwpa" label="-5%"><h4>S&amp;P 500</h4><p>Broad market return 2026 YTD</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item id="KgrdW" label="+32%"><h4>Energy</h4><p>Best-performing sector 2026 YTD</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item id="o8qwY" label="4.1x"><h4>NTM Multiple</h4><p>Public SaaS median, as of 3/20/2026 — near all-time lows</p></smart-layout-item></smart-layout></div><div><h4>Why the Selloff Is Not Indiscriminate</h4><p>The headline numbers mask a highly differentiated repricing across SaaS sub-segments. <b>Horizontal SaaS is down -35% LTM</b>. <b>Vertical SaaS is up +3%</b>. <b>Infrastructure is up +2%</b>. The market is not panicking uniformly — it is making a deliberate judgment about moat durability.</p><p>The implied long-term revenue growth rate embedded in current valuations is approximately <b>1.1%</b> — down from <b>4.7% just three months prior</b>. Investors are pricing near-zero terminal growth for the median SaaS company. For context, the current NTM multiple of 4.1x compares to a <b>December 2020 peak of 22x</b> and is approaching — though still above — the 2008 financial crisis trough of ~2.5x.</p><aside variant="warning"><p>The critical analytical frame: 85–95% of SaaS enterprise value is terminal value. A strong quarter moves less than 5% of the value. The current debate is a 10-year question about moat durability — it cannot be answered with a 90-day earnings print.</p></aside></div></columns><graph chart-type="column" show-data-labels="true" y-axis-min="-40" dimensions="{&quot;ratio&quot;:0.56}"><properties><property key="segment" label="Segment" type="label"></property><property key="ltm" label="LTM Return (%)" type="data"></property></properties><data><row><cell>Infrastructure</cell><cell>2</cell></row><row><cell>Vertical SaaS</cell><cell>3</cell></row><row><cell>S&amp;P 500</cell><cell>-5</cell></row><row><cell>Horizontal SaaS</cell><cell>-35</cell></row></data></graph></div></section><section image-layout="blank" id="4r83v35eeyy9uxe"><div class="body"><labels><label variant="solid">Chapter 4</label></labels><h1>Not All SaaS Is Equal: Moat Depth Determines Survival</h1><img id="FQhbZ" src="https://cdn.gamma.app/zc87vhr30n8uf3n/uploaded-images/VrUCV5Tsej4JmmAVcmsdH.png" source="image.custom" resize="{&quot;width&quot;:760}"><smart-layout variant="outlineBoxesWithSideLine" cell-size="15"><smart-layout-item id="_q3Jy"><h4><span text-color="#9FEED9">Vertical SaaS (+3%): The Moat Is the Data</span></h4><p>Public Vertical SaaS companies that reached IPO scale are almost definitionally both the system of record and the workflow system for their industries. AI can augment these systems — it cannot displace them without re-acquiring years of proprietary, industry-specific data accumulated through years of customer activity. The switching cost is existential, not cosmetic. This is why Vertical SaaS has held its valuation while the rest of the sector has sold off.</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item id="9CV5W"><h4><span text-color="#77C9EB">Infrastructure (+2%): AI Is a Direct Tailwind</span></h4><p>Every new AI workload — whether running on Snowflake, Datadog, MongoDB, or Cloudflare — directly expands infrastructure spend. What is displacing Horizontal SaaS is simultaneously accelerating Infrastructure. More AI deployment means more compute, more data movement, more observability requirements, and more security surface area. Infrastructure is the rare category where the AI disruption narrative is a growth accelerant, not a threat.</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item id="61Mhv"><h4><span text-color="#fac496">Horizontal SaaS (-35%): Accidentally Optimized for Replaceability</span></h4><p>Horizontal SaaS was architected to serve every industry equally — which meant integrating deeply with none. Without proprietary data or long process history, switching costs are lower. These products were largely designed to track coordination: who is doing what and when, task assignments, status updates, approval workflows. That is precisely the category of work that AI orchestration handles natively. Horizontal SaaS faces the highest displacement risk from both AI-native competitors and foundation models moving up the stack.</p></smart-layout-item></smart-layout><columns col-widths="[50,50]"><div><h4>Valuation Premium for Profitable Growth</h4><p>The market is not just penalizing AI risk — it is rewarding execution. Companies with <b>NTM revenue growth &gt;20%</b> command a median NTM revenue multiple of <b>8.1x</b>. Companies with <b>NTM revenue growth &lt;10% and FCF margin &lt;20%</b> trade at just <b>1.2x</b> — a 6.75x valuation gap for profitable growth.</p></div><div><h4>Key Risk Assessment for Incumbents</h4><table col-widths="[50,50]" table-style="bordered"><tbody><tr><td><p><b>Risk Factor</b></p></td><td><p><b>Concern Level</b></p></td></tr><tr><td><p>New AI-native startup competitors</p></td><td><p><span text-color="#C593DB"><b>HIGH</b></span></p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Foundation models moving up-stack</p></td><td><p><b>MED</b></p></td></tr><tr><td><p>SBC overhang on equity value</p></td><td><p><span text-color="#C593DB"><b>HIGH</b></span></p></td></tr><tr><td><p>DIY vibe-coding replacement</p></td><td><p><b>LOW</b></p></td></tr></tbody></table></div></columns></div></section><section image-layout="blank" id="4zjyeth4qa0u23h"><div class="body"><labels><label variant="solid">Chapter 5</label></labels><h1>CIO Behavior Is Shifting: Consolidation, Not Expansion</h1><img id="_5wXx" src="https://cdn.gamma.app/zc87vhr30n8uf3n/uploaded-images/esaO1rqA-l13J04Dwup2n.png" source="image.custom" resize="{&quot;width&quot;:760}"><columns col-widths="[40,60]"><div background-color="#1f2a5d"><smart-layout variant="stats" cell-size="10"><smart-layout-item id="zf-PK" label="54%"><h4>CIOs Consolidating</h4><p>Actively pursuing vendor consolidation</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item id="t1MnF" label="45%"><h4>AI Replacing Software</h4><p>Of AI budgets are replacing existing software budgets</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item id="uBZR5" label="3%"><h4>Expect More Vendors</h4><p>Only 3% of CIOs expect AI to lead to a larger vendor roster</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item id="BcU-Z" label="58%"><h4>AI Driving Spend</h4><p>Cite AI feature additions as #1 driver of software spend increases</p></smart-layout-item></smart-layout></div><div><h4>Categories Most at Risk for Replacement</h4><p>CIO survey data reveals a clear hierarchy of displacement risk, ordered by the percentage of respondents identifying each category as likely to be replaced by AI:</p><graph chart-type="bar" show-data-labels="true" dimensions="{&quot;ratio&quot;:0.56}"><properties><property key="category" label="Category" type="label"></property><property key="pct" label="% CIOs Identifying as At-Risk" type="data"></property></properties><data><row><cell>Customer Service Mgmt</cell><cell>26</cell></row><row><cell>Finance Ops</cell><cell>21</cell></row><row><cell>Project Mgmt</cell><cell>20</cell></row><row><cell>Salesforce Automation</cell><cell>19</cell></row><row><cell>HRIS</cell><cell>17</cell></row></data></graph><h4>Pricing Model Transition in Progress</h4><p>The seat-based SaaS pricing model is under direct pressure. <b>46% of CIOs</b> expect usage/outcome-based models to become more common. <b>35% of CIOs</b> expect flat subscriptions to decline. Seat-based models are split: <b>29% see them growing</b>, <b>29% see them declining</b> — a sign of acute uncertainty. This pricing shift directly attacks SaaS NRR mechanics and compounds the terminal value repricing in DCF models: lower growth, lower retention, lower valuation.</p></div></columns></div></section><section image-layout="blank" id="z89w11o016xmtbk"><div class="body"><labels><label variant="solid">Chapter 6</label></labels><h1>The Venture Opportunity: AI-Native Is Where Value Is Being Created</h1><img id="iai3s" src="https://cdn.gamma.app/zc87vhr30n8uf3n/uploaded-images/DD7JZHpIZ8hFOUWy8kdFy.png" source="image.custom" resize="{&quot;width&quot;:760}"><columns col-widths="[55,45]"><div><h4>ARR Velocity: Unprecedented by Historical Standards</h4><p>AI-native companies are compounding ARR faster than any prior technology era. Cursor, OpenAI, Lovable, and Anthropic are all tracking above the steepest historical ARR curves on record — outpacing the early-stage trajectories of Slack, Salesforce, and ServiceNow at comparable stages of maturity.</p><p><b>OpenAI</b>: $3.7B revenue in 2024, current run-rate $25B, projected $113B by 2028E. <b>Anthropic</b>: $0.4B in 2024, current run-rate $19B, projected $102B by 2028E. These are not venture projections — they are extrapolations from observed run-rates with strong customer commitment underpinning them.</p><aside><p>The 2026–2027 window is historically significant: cumulative enterprise value data from the Internet, Mobile, and Cloud eras consistently shows that companies founded approximately <b>4 years post-platform shift</b> capture the most total value. The ChatGPT release was November 2022. The window is open now.</p></aside></div><div background-color="#12386e"><h4>VC Funding Concentration Is Extreme</h4><p>Capital is flowing in a highly concentrated pattern that has no precedent in prior technology cycles:</p><ul><li>Top 20 enterprise software deals captured <b>44% of all VC dollars in 2025</b> — up from just 8% in 2020</li><li><b>Model Labs hold 66%</b> of enterprise value despite representing only 3% of deal count</li><li>Infrastructure holds <b>21%</b> of enterprise value; Apps hold <b>13%</b></li></ul><h4>Early-Stage Valuation Inflation</h4><p>Seed-stage median valuations spiked to <b>~$60M in Q1'26</b> — up from ~$20M in Q1'24, a 3x expansion in 24 months. Series B is pricing at ~$480M; Series C at ~$1B. The AI premium at early stages is real, compressing prospective returns and requiring disciplined entry.</p></div></columns><graph chart-type="column" show-data-labels="true" dimensions="{&quot;ratio&quot;:0.56}"><properties><property key="year" label="Year" type="label"></property><property key="openai" label="OpenAI Revenue ($B)" type="data"></property><property key="anthropic" label="Anthropic Revenue ($B)" type="data"></property></properties><data><row><cell>2024A</cell><cell>3.7</cell><cell>0.4</cell></row><row><cell>Current Run-Rate</cell><cell>25</cell><cell>19</cell></row><row><cell>2028E</cell><cell>113</cell><cell>102</cell></row></data></graph></div></section><section image-layout="blank" id="6zast5u8fktpx0i"><div class="body"><labels><label variant="solid">Chapter 7</label></labels><h1>What to Do Now: Playbook for Founders and Investors</h1><columns col-widths="[50,50]"><div background-color="#1f2a5d"><h4>For AI-Native Startups: Two Winning Strategies</h4><smart-layout variant="outlineBoxesWithLabel" cell-size="10" numbered="true"><smart-layout-item id="eg9dh"><h4>Augment the Enterprise</h4><p>Sell alongside the incumbent. Own the AI intelligence layer on top of an entrenched system of record. The incumbent retains the data and the contract — you own the intelligence. Value accrues to the intelligence layer because incumbents cannot move fast enough. Examples: Sierra/Decagon vs. Salesforce Service Cloud; Legora vs. LexisNexis. The wedge is intelligence, not data.</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item id="19wIH"><h4>Greenfield Replacement</h4><p>Build net-new in categories where incumbents have no meaningful data moat — where historical data is sparse, generic, or not proprietary. Attack the coordination-layer tools in Horizontal SaaS where switching costs are low and AI handles the core use case natively.</p></smart-layout-item></smart-layout></div><div><h4>For Incumbent SaaS Companies: 8 Imperatives</h4><smart-layout variant="processSteps" numbered="true" cell-size="10"><smart-layout-item id="O8gIv"><h4>Assume Zero, Rebuild the Case</h4><p>Mental assumption of business worthlessness — then rebuild the investment thesis from scratch with AI in the model.</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item id="kd8YA"><h4>Live the Tools</h4><p>Leadership must use AI products daily. Make AI a standing boardroom agenda item, not a QBR footnote.</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item id="PRTyG"><h4>Test Pricing Models Now</h4><p>Pilot usage/outcome-based pricing before customers demand it. Seat-based models are under structural pressure.</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item id="0wHPH"><h4>Pursue Strategic Options</h4><p>Assess M&amp;A if leverage exists. Evaluate acqui-hire value of team and data assets proactively, not reactively.</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item id="13Wum"><h4>Shorten Planning Horizons</h4><p>Compress annual planning to 6–12 month cycles. Use AI to improve margins and drive cash flow in the near term.</p></smart-layout-item></smart-layout></div></columns><columns col-widths="[50,50]"><div><h4>Vibe-Coding Economics Still Favor Buying</h4><p>The DIY threat is rated LOW for a reason. Slack costs approximately <b>$220K/year for 1,000 employees</b>. The estimated cost to replicate comparable functionality in-house is <b>$2M+ per year</b> — making DIY <b>8x more expensive and a structurally worse product</b>. The build-vs-buy math strongly favors incumbents with established products, at least at current AI capability levels.</p></div><div><h4>The Newspaper Warning: Heed the Precedent</h4><p>US newspaper stocks held stable consensus forward earnings through 2007 while stock prices declined for years before the fundamental break. The terminal value repricing happened <i>before</i> the earnings deterioration was visible in quarterly results. The lesson: <b>do not anchor strategy to near-term earnings stability as evidence that the long-run moat is intact.</b> The market is already discounting the terminal value. Founders and boards must do the same.</p></div></columns><aside variant="success"><p><b>Bottom Line:</b> The AI revolution is real and cash-flow funded. The SaaS selloff reflects legitimate terminal value uncertainty, not near-term fundamental collapse. Winners will be defined by three factors: <b>depth of data moats</b>, <b>speed of AI adoption into the product and operating model</b>, and <b>agility of pricing model transition</b>. The companies that treat today's environment as a fire drill rather than an existential signal will not survive the next five years.</p></aside></div></section><section image-layout="blank" id="y03wsybhtx6ars3"><div class="body"><h1>Sources</h1><smart-layout variant="outlineBoxes" cell-size="15"><smart-layout-item id="e8RkW"><h4>Primary Source</h4><p><a href="#">2026 Market Update — Redpoint Ventures (2026)</a></p></smart-layout-item></smart-layout></div></section>